ting into farsi first of all having  put a lot of effort into the arabic  writing system this is not going to help  me in farsi there's a few additional or  different letters in farsi but by and  large it's the same writing system  uh i want to be able to my doctor is  from iran uh shopkeepers are from iran  uh i run into farsi speakers all the  time  so  you know obviously this becomes  motivating and i often make the point  that you know how early you start to  speak depends on where you are and what  your opportunities are to speak uh if i  had been living in an arabic country for  example i would have spoken much more  arabic even with my limited level of  arabic  whereas you know having to set up  discussions via skype and stuff i want  to make sure that i have a certain level  you know of comprehension before i do  that i think i'm kind of starting to be  at that level now in my arabic  but  bear in mind that i'm retired i do  whatever i want with my time  i can putt around the garden i can go  play golf i last night we had the family  over we were swimming in the ocean  and within that i find time to indulge  my interest in languages  and an interest in languages means an  interest obviously in speaking to the  people but also connecting to that  history culture civilization and so  forth so obviously getting into arabic i  looked around and found that in my  library of books that i have bought over  time i have a book here on a history of  the arab peoples  which is interesting  and as i started into farsi then i  looked around my library again and i  found i had this book persian fire i  haven't gone very far into it  you know i tend to buy books because the  title seems interesting but it is as you  get into this book for example and and i  did and i read a bit about  uh you know cyrus  and the uh coruscant highway and and  just the extent to which  this the sort of middle east central  asia is connected  uh i mean the sort of  abrahamic religions you know  judaism christianity islam  came from a part of the world where  uh this is well before islam  where persians and semitic peoples  were and so you have the assyrians and  the  medians or whatever they were the medes  and the persians and  whatever it's all fascinating to read  about this but get a better sense of the  of the history and the geography of that  region  than you do just by you know looking at  the odd map of the world and so to me  that's fascinating and  i'm not yet we're at the level where i  could  you know read about the history of  arab  countries or  persia in their language but i'm kind of  starting on that journey and i find that  a very you know fascinating  prospect  that i can do that we have added i have  found people online to create content  for us in farsi we now have uh who is  she  which have started into we have the many  stories in farsi  we have a couple who are producing  casual conversation both at a beginner  level and a more sort of intermediate  level  all of this is gradually  going to be put up on link the only  problem with  with uh farsi is that we don't have  text-to-speech for whatever reason it's  not available in the text-to-speech  package that we use  so it's a bit of a disadvantage but what  i'm doing with farsi  you know i first have to start to get a  sense of the language so i'm going  through who is she and many stories i'm  doing it in sentence mode  and you can find sentence mode and by  the way this week  there'll be some changes to the site  that make it easier for people to find  sentence mode but with a new language  like farsi at first it's just noise to  me  uh and then i so i take it one sentence  at a time i listen over and over again  to that sentence i try to read it  i  go through like the first uh or a couple  of lessons in who she  start to see how the language works i  also have some books that i bought  living language but they're not that  helpful  um i can't absorb sort of verb  conjugations 